    <string name="settings_version">Version</string>
    <string name="privacy_text">Photos works on this device. It has no account, no server, no analytics and no advertising.\n\nThe only internet use is map images, and only if you turn on Maps. Map images come from OpenStreetMap, whose servers then learn which area you are viewing (not which photo). Photo locations themselves are read and indexed on the device only.\n\nPhoto access is used only to show your pictures. Passwords are never stored: they are used to unlock a photo and then discarded.\n\nWhen you share an unlocked original, a temporary copy is created only for that share and deleted automatically.</string>
    <string name="how_text">Each protected photo is a single file that every app can open as a normal picture, but all they see is its preview (or plain artwork, depending on your choice). Hidden after the picture is the original photo, encrypted with AES-256-GCM.\n\nTo view the original, open the photo in Photos, tap ••• and choose Continue Securely. A wrong password simply leaves the preview on screen.\n\nShare protected photos as files. Apps that recompress pictures (for example some chat apps when sending as a photo) keep only the preview.\n\nThe encryption key is derived from your password with Argon2id (%1$d MiB of memory, %2$d passes), which makes guessing passwords slow. Every file has its own random salt and keys.\n\nAny change to the file, including its preview, is detected when it is unlocked. Without the password, the original cannot be recovered. Not by Photos, and not by anyone else.\n\nThe format is documented publicly so other apps can support it.</string>
